A||||14||Will||Performance Package Fiero||||1||
Z||000000||Will||10-26-2000||03:30 PM||||Who would be interested in a Fiero Performance Package?<BR>Each one would be built to order. Available options would include the following:<P>Mustang II coil over or '88 front suspension conversion (rod ends or poly bushings, not rubber) The mustang II suspension would be MUCH cheaper than Ryane's Slalom kit.<P>Short-long A-arm conversion with coil overs for rear suspension, also poly bushings or rod-ends<P>12 5/8 brake rotors all around with 4 pot calipers<P>17 x 8 wheels up front with 245/45-ZR17 tires<BR>17 x 9 wheels in rear with 275/40-ZR17 (corvette size) tires<P>Engine swap of buyer's choice: 3.4 pushrod, 3.4 TDC, Northstar, SD4, Quad 4<BR>Probably not an iron block V8 though. The point of this package is handling.<P>All of the parts would be available separately or in kit form. Any combination of options could be mix-and-matched.<P>What kind of interest would there be for this?||||reg||1
Z||000001||william_thorniii||10-26-2000||03:41 PM||||Will,<P>Those sound exactly what I have been working on rounding up on my own!  Too bad you didn't offer these 6 months ago.  Perhaps by the time I am ready for the Northstar... (dreaming)<P>Although similar to what I said earlier in another post... it is easy to be interested but not be in the market.  I feel that these are things that 90% of us as Fiero owners will be interested in, but only 10% are in a position to purchase.  That is just my opinion though.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<p>[This message has been edited by william_thorniii (edited 10-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||10-26-2000||04:01 PM||||What kind of prices would we be talking for the full suspension package, minus the wheels & tires? (I already have some in mind.)<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000003||trevorp||10-26-2000||04:49 PM||||Will,<BR>  Even though I haven't managed to get my Fiero yet, these are the kind of things I'm going to be doing, so count me in as interested.<P>  Also, will you be offering any transaxle swaps?  I.e. I have the chance to get an automatic, but I'd really rather have a stick.  I know it's going to be a pain to do the swap, but if I had all the parts, it's something I woulnd't mind doing.<P>Thanks in advance,<BR>  -Trevor<P>-----<BR>(No sig available.  Don't have a car to take pics of)||||reg||6
Z||000004||count_iblis||10-26-2000||04:51 PM||||i would also be<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||2xsess||10-26-2000||07:27 PM||||Will, the pricing will make all the difference, I agree with what WTIII said, most of us would love to do all those things but can't afford it. <P>Also location would be a key element, As far as I know there aren't any of these type of shops here on the east coast. Correct me if I'm wrong here guys. I know there are a bunch in CA and the midwest. And I know Ed Parks in over in Alabama. Which as far as I know he is the only Fiero specific shop in the east.<P><BR>Just my .02<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||Songman||10-26-2000||08:22 PM||||It all sounds great! What do you have in mind?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||10-27-2000||03:30 PM||||trevorp:<P>     As long as you can get your hands on a stick shift fiero, there is nothing special required for the transaxle swap. <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A>  sells many of the brackets you will need if you can't get a stick shift fiero. The clutch hydraulic line can be faked with high pressure hose or tubing if an original is not available.<BR>I believe the clutch MC and SC are still available new.<BR>The only problems I foresee if you can't find a donor car in a junkyard is the clutch pedal bracket & clutch/brake pedal pivot pin, and  the shifter.<P>WTT3:<P>     I wrote a post a while back about the mechanical nature of a N* swap. I don't know if you saw it or not. Go look it up: "A northstar swap is easy as pie"    <P>Everybody else:<P>     I am on the east coast, in central western VA (Shenandoah Valley).<BR>     The package pricing would obviously depend on what options it included.<BR>     Mustang II front suspension would be a bolt in replacement for the stock Fiero cross member, similar to Ryane's Slalom kit, but costing between $1000 and $1500 (totally ball park/off the cuff guess, as I haven't finished building one yet) if you install. It could obviously be shipped anywhere in the country.<BR>     Short-long A-arm rear suspension would be in the $250-500 range if you install, plus whatever coil overs you want to use. It would reuse the stock lower control arm and hub carrier, and lose the toe link. It would most likely require welding the upper control arm inner pivots to the frame rail. Other than that, it will be bolt in.<BR>     Brakes: if you install, it will cost whatever it costs me to fabricate the caliper mount brackets plus the cost of the calipers and rotors.<BR>     Engine swaps would be competitively priced.<P>     All kit options would be designed to be as bolt-in as possible.<BR>     All prices are completely ball park at this time.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||william_thorniii||10-27-2000||04:04 PM||||Will,<P>I remember all of your previous posts as they have intrigued me immensely.  I too am a DOHC nut and therefore am eager to read about both the TDC and N*.  <P>The question I have for you is this... have you figured out the ECM yet?  What are you going to be required to do to make this all work?  <P>Thanks, <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000009||Will||10-28-2000||04:25 AM||||I'm still working on that.<P>The easiest and most probable solution is to use the Easterly chip.<P>The coolest solution would be to use an LS1 computer (Corvette, Camaro, Firebird), as it is much more mainstream than either the cadillac computer or the easterly chip, and will have reprogramming software available sometime next year.<P>According to the '95 shop manual, the crankshaft and camshaft position sensors on the northstar engine send their signals to the ignition module (the box on which the coil pack sits), which converts it to a much simpler 24x pulse train for crank sensor and 1/2x cam sensor to send to the ECM. If there is a similar setup for the LS1, then using an LS1 computer to run a northstar would be childishly simple. I doubt it's quite that simple, but I don't doubt at all that it can be done with a black box signal translator. If I can find out what the LS1 pulse train looks like...<BR>     I'm going down to the local pontiac dealer sometime soon to look at whatever LS1 shop manual I can find.<P>     If I can use an LS1 computer, it shouldn't be hard to reprogram it to deal with boost, and use a pair of water cooled garret T-3's with electronic wastegates. Hehehe. <BR>10 psi of boost and some cylinder head work should easily net ~ 440 rwhp. This would have to be on a MY 2000 N* because of the improved combustion chamber design for better octane tolerance.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Wannabe||10-28-2000||09:24 AM||||Sounds promising. Since Ryane has dropped their offerings, I've been working on some simple solutions to completely upgrading the front and rear suspensions. Your Mustang II idea sounds good, as it would save the hassle of designing and having somone build a new subframe. I was figuring on using off the shelf upper and lower arms and ball joints and having a local rod shop fabricate the subframe to my specs. The toughes part so far has been finding a suitable spindle. If you can really bring a package together for those kinds of prices, or even a bit more, it'll be hard to beat. Any idea how long ot migh take to get them ready? Please keep us posted!||||reg||14
Z||000011||stimpy||10-28-2000||11:13 AM||||I may need some correction here, but wasn't the Mustang II basically a Pinto? Isn't two large kind off a bunch to swap out a Chevette front end for a Pinto?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> hehe...<P>Actually I realize that Pinto's actually were pretty god handling little cars, and lots of aftermarket products are available as Mustang II racks are very popular in the kit car marketplace.  I just want to get everyone ready for the dumbass comments sure to come from the unwashed publick!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||10-28-2000||11:08 PM||||Wannabe:<P>     You wanna have your cake and eat it too? A good job cheap won't be quick. I have a huge workload this semester, and will again next semester. Working protoypes might be on the road as early as the middle of next year.<P>stimpy:<P>I was wondering about the opinion of the unwashed publick on using Ford suspension in a Pontiac?!? Arrrrrgh!<P>Mustang II IFS is the suspension of choice for street rodders because it has good camber characteristics, good anti-dive, and zero bump steer. There is a large aftermarket built around it. Check out <A HREF="http://www.heidts.com" TARGET=_blank>www.heidts.com</A>  for example.<BR>     A friend of my father's is building a car with MII IFS. I will work with him to make jigs to build a stand alone crossmember and my own A-arms and spindles.<BR>     The spindle would accept stock Fiero brakes so that expensive brake and wheel upgrades are not required at the same time as an expensive suspension upgrade. Adapter brackets for caliper mounting would allow the use of other calipers (wilwood, corvette, etc.)<BR>     The MII suspension will lower the car 3/4" with respect to stock. When used with 245/45-ZR17 front tires, which are 1.5" taller than the stock 15" tires, the car will have stock ground clearance.<BR>     Yes, the MII suspension was also used in Pintos, although the MII was a more attractive car with more powerful engines available.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Wannabe||10-29-2000||06:56 PM||||I never said it had to be quick, just wondering how long you think it might take. Unfortunately, middle of next year will be too late for my needs (wants  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">), and I fully expect to have to pay more to get it done before that. I'll probably spend most of next season getting it dialed in though.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Wannabe||10-29-2000||09:17 PM||||Will, why not stick closer to the exisitng Mustang II components, to keep thing simpler. Wouldn't it be easier to adapt available control arms and spindles? That way the only thing that really needs to be developed is a subframe that allows the existing, proven componenets to bolt into the Fiero. This may not apeal to Fiero/GM purists, but those who are looking for an affordable way to seriously improve the handling of their Fiero for racing or auto-x will probably welcome the chance. The reality is that Ford got the geometry right on the Mustang II while GM did not on the Chevette. It's not too often you hear of hot rodders adapting the Chevette front end to their cars. I'm sure that if the '88 Fiero had made it into the 100,000 plus production numbers, then that would also have been a popular front suspension for hot rodders.||||reg||1
